我们搬回了东部城市郊区的小镇，

154
00:07:19.020 --> 00:07:25.740
backyard, picket fence, or at least I'll have some semi-helpful grandparents and a few old friends
有后院、木栅栏，或者至少我能有一些稍微能帮上忙的祖父母和几个老朋友

155
00:07:25.740 --> 00:07:27.109
to keep me sane.
来让我保持理智。

156
00:07:27.109 --> 00:07:34.380
We have a second child, Darcy, also wonderful, also challenging, who is neurotypical,
我们有了第二个孩子，达西，同样很棒，也极具挑战性，是个发育正常儿童，

157
00:07:35.100 --> 00:07:37.979
another not-at-all clunky term.
又一个一点也不生硬的词。

158
00:07:37.979 --> 00:07:43.180
And with that, I fully, finally and irretrievably become
随着这一切，我彻底、最终且无可挽回地成为了

159
00:07:43.900 --> 00:07:44.206
mom.
妈妈。

160
00:07:44.206 --> 00:07:51.100
More years paSt. Rob continues to work in Hollywood, but now from New York, which is hard.
多年过去了。罗布继续在好莱坞工作，但现在从纽约办公，这很不容易。

161
00:07:51.100 --> 00:07:52.568
This is all pre-zoom.
这都是在 Zoom 普及之前的事了。

162
00:07:52.568 --> 00:07:57.740
He flies to LA a lot, goes missing for weeks, months at a time for shoots,
他经常飞往洛杉矶，一拍就是几个星期、几个月不见人影，

163
00:07:57.740 --> 00:08:03.660
and the one thing you can 100% count on, more than death or taxes or an abacus,
而有一件事你可以百分之百指望，比死亡、税收或算盘还要确定，

164
00:08:03.660 --> 00:08:08.380
is that the moment the wheels of Rob's plane leave the runway, one of the kids will get explosive
那就是罗布的飞机轮子刚离开跑道，其中一个孩子就会得突发性

165
00:08:08.380 --> 00:08:14.700
diarrhea or head lice or both will get both, at the same time, all of which I will get to,
腹泻或长头虱，或者两个孩子同时得这两种病，而所有这些我全都会沾上，

166
00:08:14.700 --> 00:08:24.380
but Rob will dodge, triumphantly returning the moment it's all over must be nice, which is also
但罗布却能躲过，并在一切刚结束时凯旋归来，真好啊，这也是

167
00:08:24.380 --> 00:08:30.940
the inner command I repeat to myself to avoid strangling Rob when he comes back home with a tan.
我为了避免在罗布晒得一身古铜色回家时掐死他，而在内心不断对自己重复的咒语。

168
00:08:32.540 --> 00:08:38.220
It's all a little easier with family support nearby, but if I'm honest, I still feel like a
有了附近的家人支持，一切稍微容易了一些，但说实话，我还是觉得

169
00:08:38.220 --> 00:08:40.948
single parent half the time.
自己有一半时间像个单亲妈妈。

170
00:08:40.948 --> 00:08:42.994
Even more years pass.
更多的岁月流逝了。

171
00:08:42.994 --> 00:08:44.651
The kids grow up.
孩子们长大了。

172
00:08:44.651 --> 00:08:45.820
To be clear,
坦白说，

173
00:08:46.460 --> 00:08:48.226
I adore my children.
我深爱我的孩子们。

174
00:08:48.226 --> 00:08:53.257
But by the time they're teenagers, I'm fucking losing it.
但当他们到了青少年时期，我他妈的快要疯了。

175
00:08:53.257 --> 00:08:54.140
My bad-ass
我那厉害的

176
00:08:54.140 --> 00:09:00.220
journalism career is fossilized in amber, like that little Jurassic Park mosquito with its
我的新闻职业生涯就像琥珀里的化石一样，就像《侏罗纪公园》里那只

177
00:09:00.220 --> 00:09:02.413
proboscis full of dinosaur DNA.
吻部吸满恐龙DNA的小蚊子。

178
00:09:02.413 --> 00:09:06.940
All of the genetic blueprints for a world stomping Tyrannosaurus
重现一个暴龙横行的世界的所有基因蓝图

179
00:09:06.940 --> 00:09:13.660
just sitting there, collecting duSt. Meanwhile, for Emmet, the end of high school and his uncertain
就那样静静地躺在那里，积满灰尘。与此同时，对埃米特来说，高中生活的结束和他作为一个

180
00:09:13.660 --> 00:09:19.500
future as an adult with developmental disabilities, despite being on the higher functioning end
有发育障碍的成年人不确定的未来——尽管他在学术上属于功能较好的一端，

181
00:09:19.500 --> 00:09:23.786
academically, I mix whenever a specialist says this about my son.
每当专家这样评价我儿子时，我总是喜忧参半。

182
00:09:23.786 --> 00:09:25.500
It sounds somehow boastful
这听起来既像是在夸耀

183
00:09:25.500 --> 00:09:30.352
and diminishing at the same time, loom large on the horizon.
又带有一点贬低，宛如一片阴影笼罩在天边。

184
00:09:30.352 --> 00:09:32.940
Rob and I are so lucky to have a
我和罗伯非常庆幸能为埃米特

185
00:09:33.020 --> 00:09:36.504
wonderful, loving, younger sibling for Emmet in Darcy.
生下达西这样一个乖巧、有爱心的小妹妹。

186
00:09:36.504 --> 00:09:39.020
And yet we are so aware that she should
然而我们也深知，

187
00:09:39.020 --> 00:09:43.340
not feel it's her job in adulthood to be responsible for her brother once we're gone.
等我们离去后，她不该把照顾哥哥当作自己成年后的责任。

188
00:09:44.460 --> 00:09:46.168
Then I have an idea.
然后我产生了一个想法。

189
00:09:46.168 --> 00:09:50.780
It takes me two years of excruciatingly slow progress,
我花了两年时间，进展慢得令人痛苦，

190
00:09:51.500 --> 00:09:56.739
but I write a memoir about my journey as Emmet's mom, called Now What?
但我写了一本关于我作为埃米特母亲的心路历程的回忆录，名为《现在怎么办？》

191
00:09:56.739 --> 00:09:58.460
The title refers to the
书名指的是

192
00:09:58.460 --> 00:10:04.380
giant cliff anyone raising a child with a disability is headed toward a cliff called adulthood.
任何抚养残疾儿童的人都将面临的巨大悬崖——一个被称为成年期的悬崖。

193
00:10:05.020 --> 00:10:09.606
What happens after your atypical kid ages out of the school system?
当你的非典型孩子超出了学校系统的年龄限制后会发生什么？

194
00:10:09.606 --> 00:10:11.660
We fancy ourselves an advanced
我们自诩为先进文明，

195
00:10:11.660 --> 00:10:16.320
civilization and yet we have shockingly few good answers to this question.
然而对于这个问题，我们竟然找不出几个好的答案。

196
00:10:16.320 --> 00:10:17.580
And then there's the
还有另一个层面的

197
00:10:17.580 --> 00:10:19.755
other aspect of the Now What?
“现在怎么办？”

198
00:10:19.755 --> 00:10:20.430
question.
这个问题。

199
00:10:20.430 --> 00:10:21.630
The parent part.
也就是父母的层面。

200
00:10:21.630 --> 00:10:24.780
As in, you just spent the last two decades
也就是说，你刚刚度过了过去的二十年

201
00:10:24.860 --> 00:10:31.260
grinding to get your kid ready for a life of semi-independence so that one day you can keep
拼尽全力把孩子抚养成人，让他过上某种半独立的生计，好让你终有一天能赴约

202
00:10:31.260 --> 00:10:36.042
the non-negotiable appointment at the end of your life where you, you know, die.
那个生命终点无法商量的契约——也就是，你懂的，走向死亡。

203
00:10:36.042 --> 00:10:36.700
Never fully
却又从未真正

204
00:10:36.700 --> 00:10:40.267
confronting the fact you've still got five more decades to fill.
去直面这样一个事实：你的人生其实还有整整五十年要去打发。

205
00:10:40.267 --> 00:10:41.660
Even though you no longer
即便你早已

206
00:10:41.660 --> 00:10:46.300
remember who the hell you are, what the hell you want, or what the hell you did before all this.
想不起自己究竟是谁、到底想要什么，或者在这一切发生之前你都干过些什么。

207
00:10:47.020 --> 00:10:51.580
So now you're just left stumbling around in the dark wondering, now what?
所以现在，你只能在黑暗中摸索前行，心里纳闷：接下来该怎么办？

208
00:10:51.580 --> 00:10:59.420
To answer that question, I open a local business called Cafe Everyone, where my son and other
为了回答这个问题，我开了一家名叫“每个人咖啡馆”的本地小店，我和我儿子以及其他

209
00:10:59.420 --> 00:11:06.460
young adults with disabilities can work, have some structure, and hopefully find meaning in community.
患有残疾的年轻人们可以在这里工作、建立生活规律，并希望能在社区里找到人生的意义。

210
00:11:07.260 --> 00:11:14.260
Despite my not knowing what the hell I'm doing, even a little, the damn thing works.
尽管我压根不知道自己在搞什么鬼，哪怕一点都不知道，但这破店居然还真开成了。

211
00:11:14.260 --> 00:11:15.260
Not a smash,
算不上大获成功，

212
00:11:15.260 --> 00:11:18.400
but a viable business with me as the manager.
但它是一个由我担任经理的可行商业项目。

213
00:11:18.400 --> 00:11:21.820
The catch, the better things go at Cafe Everyone,
问题在于，“每个人咖啡馆”的情况越好，

214
00:11:21.820 --> 00:11:26.979
the less Emmet needs me there, which is the whole point for me not to be there.
埃梅特对我的依赖就越少，而这正是我不该在那里的全部意义所在。

215
00:11:26.979 --> 00:11:28.220
And so I step back.
于是我退居幕后。

216
00:11:28.860 --> 00:11:35.580
It's odd pouring so much into an endeavor whose ultimate success depends entirely on your absence.
把这么大心血倾注在一个其最终成功完全取决于你是否缺席的事业上，感觉还真是挺奇妙的。

217
00:11:36.460 --> 00:11:39.846
But I guess that's no different than parenthood in general.
不过我想这跟普通的父母之道也没什么两样。

218
00:11:39.846 --> 00:11:41.740
We also buy Emmet a group home to
我们还给埃梅特买了一套集体宿舍，

219
00:11:41.740 --> 00:11:46.940
live in with three high school friends, now coworkers, so they can be as independent as possible,
让他和三个高中时期的朋友（现在也是同事）住在一起，好让他们尽可能地独立生活，

220
00:11:47.660 --> 00:11:50.320
which is to say still not all the way.
也就是说，依然无法做到完全独立。

221
00:11:50.320 --> 00:11:53.260
Emmet is the most verbal and social roomy,
埃梅特是室友里最能说会道、最爱社交的一个，

222
00:11:53.260 --> 00:11:55.351
but they all have different challenges.
但他们每个人都有各自不同的挑战。

223
00:11:55.351 --> 00:11:58.460
We employ a rotating staff of direct support professionals
我们雇用了轮班制的直接支持专业人员，

224
00:11:58.460 --> 00:12:00.425
to help them run their lives all week.
来协助他们打理一周的生活。

225
00:12:00.425 --> 00:12:03.580
Each night, one of the staffers sleeps over to be on hand for
每天晚上，都会有一名工作人员留宿，以便应对

226
00:12:03.580 --> 00:12:04.276
emergencies.
紧急情况。

227
00:12:04.276 --> 00:12:09.500
The other roomies parents chip in what they can, but the real money comes from me and Rob,
其他室友的父母也尽其所能地出资出力，但真正的资金还是我和罗布出的，

228
00:12:09.500 --> 00:12:12.793
and really from Rob, so he deserves a lot of credit.
其实主要还是罗布出的，所以他居功至伟。

229
00:12:12.793 --> 00:12:15.580
He took on extra rewrite gigs to pay for it,
他接了额外的剧本重写工作来支付这笔费用，

230
00:12:15.580 --> 00:12:18.767
punching up some truly crappy movie scripts.
帮一些实在烂透了的电影剧本润色。

231
00:12:18.767 --> 00:12:19.998
But it all works.
但这一切总算运作起来了。

232
00:12:19.998 --> 00:12:22.460
Emmet is happy, and my young adult
埃梅特很开心，我这个

233
00:12:22.460 --> 00:12:26.156
with a disability has a place in the world.
患有残疾的年轻儿子在这个世界上总算有了一席之地。

234
00:12:26.156 --> 00:12:27.274
No easy feat.
这可绝非易事。

235
00:12:27.274 --> 00:12:30.540
And with that, I have the happy ending
至此，我这本小小回忆录

236
00:12:30.540 --> 00:12:32.340
of my little memoir.
也算迎来了大团圆结局。

237
00:12:32.340 --> 00:12:36.300
Now, I have zero expectations for this book.
话说回来，我对这本书完全没有任何不切实际的期望。

238
00:12:36.300 --> 00:12:39.180
It's mainly an excuse to sit and
它主要只是个让我能坐下来思考的借口，

239
00:12:39.180 --> 00:12:43.740
think, to have a small thing that's mine amid the daily avalanche of my family's needs.
在应付家庭日常琐事的狂轰滥炸之余，能拥有一件属于我自己的小事。

240
00:12:44.780 --> 00:12:47.834
From my old life as a journalist, I know a small publisher.
凭借我过去当记者的经历，我认识一家小型出版商。

241
00:12:47.834 --> 00:12:49.180
I send them my manuscript.
我把书稿发给了他们。

242
00:12:49.900 --> 00:12:52.106
They like it enough to publish it.
他们挺喜欢，决定出版。

243
00:12:52.106 --> 00:12:54.248
The book comes out and does okay.
书出版了，反响还行。

244
00:12:54.248 --> 00:12:55.740
A few nice mentions and
在残疾人社区相关的媒体和刊物上

245
00:12:55.740 --> 00:12:58.550
publications aimed at the disability community.
得到了几处不错的提及。

246
00:12:58.550 --> 00:13:01.420
A couple warm reviews and magazines appreciative
收获了几篇温暖的评论，以及其他像我一样的父母

247
00:13:01.420 --> 00:13:03.294
notes from other parents like me.
发来的感谢便条。

248
00:13:03.294 --> 00:13:05.337
Doesn't sell too many copies though.
不过书并没有卖出太多册。

249
00:13:05.337 --> 00:13:06.700
The publisher sends me a
出版商给我寄来了一束花，

250
00:13:06.700 --> 00:13:09.337
polite, nice work note with some flowers.
并附上了一张礼貌且赞赏工作表现的便条。

251
00:13:09.337 --> 00:13:13.260
But my phone doesn't exactly start ringing about a follow-up.
但我的电话并没有因为要谈后续而响个不停。

252
00:13:14.380 --> 00:13:16.732
And that's that, which is fine.
事情就这样过去了，倒也无所谓。

253
00:13:16.732 --> 00:13:19.008
A little disappointing, maybe.
或许稍微有点让人失望吧。

254
00:13:19.008 --> 00:13:21.740
I'd fantasize the book might spark a
我曾幻想着这本书能为我的生活开启

255
00:13:21.740 --> 00:13:27.590
new chapter in my life, or at least defibrillate my dormant journalism career.
新篇章，或者至少能给沉寂的新闻记者生涯来个电击除颤。

256
00:13:27.590 --> 00:13:28.940
But in a desert of
但在职业不得志的沙漠里

257
00:13:28.940 --> 00:13:33.236
career unfulfillment, a small drop of water is better than none.
有一小滴水总比什么都没有强。

258
00:13:33.236 --> 00:13:34.780
I go back to my day job
我回归了日常工作

259
00:13:34.860 --> 00:13:38.860
supporting everybody else while vaguely resenting how much of my adult life has been devoted to
支持着周围的每一个人，同时又隐隐怨恨着自己成年后的大部分时光都耗费在了

260
00:13:38.860 --> 00:13:40.605
supporting everybody else.
支持周围人这件事情上。

261
00:13:40.605 --> 00:13:45.100
My book somehow gets into the hands of world-famous actress Merritt
不知怎么的，我的书落到了世界知名女演员梅里特·伯克希尔的手里

262
00:13:45.100 --> 00:13:52.060
Berkshire, who, as you may know, created the largest celebrity book club on the planet.
如你所知，她创建了地球上最大的名人读书俱乐部。

263
00:13:52.780 --> 00:13:58.380
Her millions of fans would devour the 1994 Toledo phone book if she said it was a page-turner.
只要她说1994年的托莱多电话簿引人入胜，她的千万粉丝就会疯狂抢购。

264
00:13:59.340 --> 00:14:04.537
Out of nowhere, Merritt finds my memoir and loves it.
毫无征兆地，梅里特发现了我的回忆录并爱不释手。

265
00:14:04.537 --> 00:14:08.460
She even shoots a weepy video of herself
她甚至拍了一段自己泪流满面地

266
00:14:08.460 --> 00:14:09.669
finishing reading it.
读完这本书的视频。

267
00:14:09.669 --> 00:14:13.180
Apparently, the final page selfie is a meme Merritt invented.
显然，最后一页的自拍是梅里特发明的梗。

268
00:14:13.180 --> 00:14:18.140
Crying equals buy this book, nose crinkle equals don't bother.
哭泣等于买这本书，皱鼻子等于别费劲了。

269
00:14:18.140 --> 00:14:20.380
My book's final page has her
我书的最后一页上，她哭得梨花带雨

270
00:14:20.380 --> 00:14:24.153
ugly crying in the least ugly, most charming way possible.
却又以一种最不丑陋、最迷人的方式展现出来。

271
00:14:24.153 --> 00:14:26.300
If you've ever seen her rom-coms,
如果你看过她的浪漫喜剧

272
00:14:26.300 --> 00:14:30.060
one of which Rob even punched up, you know exactly what I'm talking about.
其中有一部罗布还参与润色过，你就完全明白我在说什么了。

273
00:14:30.780 --> 00:14:37.260
Through sobs, Merritt announces, I have never been more moved by another human being's journey
梅里特抽泣着宣布：“我从未被另一个人的心路历程如此感动过

274
00:14:37.260 --> 00:14:40.509
than by Paige McConnell's memoir, now what?
比被佩姬·麦康奈尔的回忆录《现在怎么办？》还要感动。”

275
00:14:40.509 --> 00:14:44.060
Guys, I'll tell you now what, go buy this book.
伙计们，我现在告诉你们“怎么办”，去买这本书。

276
00:14:47.140 --> 00:14:51.860
Merritt posts this across all her socials, as the kids say, I guess.
梅里特把这个发到了她所有的社交平台上，正如现在的年轻人所说的那样吧。

277
00:14:52.580 --> 00:14:56.580
And that is when my tiny little book explodes.
然后，我那本小小的书就彻底火了。

278
00:14:56.580 --> 00:15:02.340
Shoots up all the lists, becomes a number one New York Times bestseller.
各大畅销书排行榜飙升，成为了《纽约时报》畅销书榜首。

279
00:15:02.340 --> 00:15:04.070
Stays there for 28 weeks.
并在那里蝉联了28周。

280
00:15:04.070 --> 00:15:07.460
Suddenly, my phone won't stop ringing interviews,
突然间，我的电话响个不停，采访、

281
00:15:07.460 --> 00:15:12.340
magazine features, an invitation to be honored at the National Down Syndrome Society's
杂志专题报道、受邀出席美国唐氏综合症协会的

282
00:15:12.340 --> 00:15:13.492
annual gala.
年度慈善晚会并接受表彰。

283
00:15:13.492 --> 00:15:14.260
So real.
太不真实了。

284
00:15:14.260 --> 00:15:21.460
But by far, the most bonkers call of all comes from none other than Merritt
但到目前为止，最疯狂的电话莫过于来自

285
00:15:21.460 --> 00:15:24.344
Berkshire herself.
梅里特·伯克希尔本人。

286
00:15:24.344 --> 00:15:27.709
She wants to meet me.
她想见我。

287
00:15:27.709 --> 00:15:28.350
Rob.
罗布。

288
00:15:29.870 --> 00:15:31.900
I met my wife at a Mets game.
我和我妻子是在一场大都会队的比赛中认识的。

289
00:15:31.900 --> 00:15:36.590
We were both recent college grads living in NYC, barely employed at
我们俩都是刚毕业不久、住在纽约的大学生，在各自职业阶梯的最底层勉强糊口

290
00:15:36.590 --> 00:15:41.310
the bottom rung of our respective career ladders, while also juggling the far more important gig
同时还要兼顾一项更重要的差事

291
00:15:41.310 --> 00:15:44.830
of getting day drunk with our friends at a Mets game on a random Tuesday afternoon.
那就是在一个普通的周二下午，和朋友们在大都会队的比赛里喝个酩酊大醉。

292
00:15:45.790 --> 00:15:50.590
Paige was the most beautiful woman in the stadium, which is damning with faint praise since the stadium
佩姬是球场里最漂亮的女人，虽然这么说赞美力度有限

293
00:15:50.590 --> 00:15:55.710
was 20% full for an inconsequential late April contest against the Milwaukee Brewers.
因为这场无关紧要的四月下旬对阵密尔沃基酿酒人队的比赛，上座率只有百分之二十。

294
00:15:56.670 --> 00:15:58.825
But she was beautiful, I mean.
但我是说，她确实很美。

295
00:15:58.825 --> 00:16:02.990
My buddies were, as ever, talking shit loudly heckling the
我的哥们儿们一如既往地在大声胡扯，坐在百事可乐标志下方的廉价座位上

296
00:16:02.990 --> 00:16:06.550
Brewers right fielder from our cheap seats beneath the Pepsi sign.
对酿酒人队的右外野手大加嘲讽。

297
00:16:06.550 --> 00:16:07.790
From the row behind us,
从我们后排的座位上

298
00:16:07.790 --> 00:16:09.981
Paige and her friends joined in.
佩姬和她的朋友们也加入了进来。

299
00:16:09.981 --> 00:16:14.430
This poor outfielder, Jeremy Bernitz, had done absolutely nothing
这个可怜的外野手杰里米·伯尼茨明明什么都没做错

300
00:16:14.430 --> 00:16:17.299
wrong, but we were all tearing him a new one anyway.
但我们当时都在疯狂地痛骂他。

301
00:16:17.299 --> 00:16:19.230
After she accidentally spilled half
在佩吉不小心把半杯啤酒

302
00:16:19.230 --> 00:16:23.550
a beer on my head, Paige and I started bonding over some clever Bernitz wordplay.
倒在我头上之后，我和她开始通过一些巧妙的伯尼茨双关语熟络起来。

303
00:16:24.110 --> 00:16:25.359
Jeremy burnt tits.
杰里米·烧了胸部。

304
00:16:25.359 --> 00:16:26.747
Jeremy burned shits.
杰里米烧了一堆屎。

305
00:16:26.747 --> 00:16:30.910
Eventually, Paige got the whole section singing the diarrhea
最终，佩吉带着整个区的观众唱起了腹泻

306
00:16:30.910 --> 00:16:31.212
song.
之歌。

307
00:16:31.212 --> 00:16:35.870
When you're standing in right field and the poop starts to congeal, diarrhea,
当你在右外野站立，便便开始凝固，腹泻，

308
00:16:35.870 --> 00:16:38.211
uh, uh, diarrhea, uh, uh.
呃，呃，腹泻，呃，呃。

309
00:16:38.211 --> 00:16:40.740
Who was this magical woman?
这个神奇的女人是谁？

310
00:16:40.740 --> 00:16:43.550
At this point, Jeremy Bernitz,
就在这时，杰里米·伯尼茨——

311
00:16:43.550 --> 00:16:48.190
a solid journeyman player who again had done nothing wrong, looked up at us,
一个踏实的老兵球员，他又没做错任何事——抬头看着我们，

312
00:16:48.190 --> 00:16:51.793
shrugged his shoulders and called out, plaintively, why?
耸了耸肩膀，哀怨地喊道：“为什么？”

313
00:16:51.793 --> 00:16:54.110
Paige and I just burst out laughing.
我和佩吉顿时放声大笑。

314
00:16:54.670 --> 00:16:57.512
Turned out, she had the greatest laugh of all time.
事实证明，她拥有有史以来最棒的笑声。

315
00:16:57.512 --> 00:16:59.630
By the seventh inning stretch, I was a
到了第七局中场的伸展休息时间，我已经是

316
00:16:59.630 --> 00:16:59.954
goner.
彻底沦陷了。

317
00:16:59.954 --> 00:17:03.408
As with most days that changed your life, I wasn't expecting it.
就像大多数改变你人生的日子一样，我毫无心理准备。

318
00:17:03.408 --> 00:17:04.110
This was just
这本来只应该是

319
00:17:04.110 --> 00:17:07.683
supposed to be some silly, forgettable Tuesday in my twenties.
我二十几岁时一个愚蠢、会被淡忘的星期二。

320
00:17:07.683 --> 00:17:09.470
But an amazing girl named Paige
但一个名叫佩吉的奇妙女孩

321
00:17:09.470 --> 00:17:12.492
happened to be skipping work for a friend's birthday.
恰好为了朋友的生日而旷工。

322
00:17:12.492 --> 00:17:14.830
And fate happened to sit her right behind
而命运恰好让她坐在了

323
00:17:14.910 --> 00:17:15.043
me.
我身后。

324
00:17:15.043 --> 00:17:19.310
And then fate spilled a beer on my head just to make sure I didn't miss what was happening here,
然后命运在我头上洒了杯啤酒，确保我不会错过眼前正在发生的事，

325
00:17:19.870 --> 00:17:21.851
which was me meeting my wife.
那就是我遇到了我的妻子。

326
00:17:21.851 --> 00:17:25.950
After we got married, Paige and I moved from New York to LA,
结婚后，我和佩吉从纽约搬到了洛杉矶，

327
00:17:25.950 --> 00:17:29.310
where I eked out a living for a couple of years selling scripts that never got produced.
我在那里靠卖那些永远拍不出来的剧本勉强维持生计了好几年。

328
00:17:30.430 --> 00:17:31.865
Then, a game changer.
接着，迎来了转机。

329
00:17:31.865 --> 00:17:36.990
I wrote a script about two hardcore baseball fans who meet and fall in love
我写了一个剧本，讲的是两个铁杆棒球迷在纽约大都会队和洋基队的世界大赛期间

330
00:17:36.990 --> 00:17:42.590
during a world series in New York between the Mets and Yankees, a baseball rom-com called
相遇并坠入爱河，这是一部棒球浪漫喜剧，名叫

331
00:17:42.670 --> 00:17:43.790
Subway Series.
《地铁大战》。

332
00:17:43.790 --> 00:17:50.670
The man, a die-hard Mets fan, and the woman, a die-hard Yankees fan, meet at game one,
男主角是个大都会队的死忠，女主角是个洋基队的死忠，他们在第一场比赛相遇，

333
00:17:50.670 --> 00:17:56.830
trash talk each other's teams, spar and flirt throughout the seven-game barn burner, and ultimately,
互相垃圾话对方支持的球队，在七场精彩激烈的比赛中针锋相对、调情，最终，

334
00:17:56.830 --> 00:17:58.326
spoilers fall in love.
剧透预警，坠入爱河。

335
00:17:58.326 --> 00:18:03.630
It's basically when Harry Met Sally meets Major League meets Romeo and Juliet.
这基本上就是《当哈利遇到莎莉》碰上《大联盟》再结合《罗密欧与朱丽叶》。

336
00:18:03.630 --> 00:18:08.110
The stark cross lovers from Warring Factions part, not the tragic double suicide part.
这对来自交战派系的死对头情侣的分离，不是那种悲剧的双双殉情的部分。

337
00:18:08.750 --> 00:18:11.289
The studio made me cut that cowards.
制片厂逼我把那段删了，这群懦夫。

338
00:18:11.289 --> 00:18:15.310
The movie was inspired by Paige since baseball was a huge
这部电影的灵感来自佩吉，因为棒球是我们自己

339
00:18:15.310 --> 00:18:16.946
part of our own origin story.
爱情起源故事的重要组成部分。

340
00:18:16.946 --> 00:18:20.670
The script sparked a bidding war between several major studios and
这个剧本引发了几大好莱坞制片厂之间的竞标大战，

341
00:18:20.670 --> 00:18:24.910
sold for a life-altering amount of money back when that could still happen in Hollywood.
并以一笔能改变人生的巨额资金售出，在当时好莱坞还偶尔能发生这种事的时候。

342
00:18:25.710 --> 00:18:30.549
The up-and-coming rom-com director Nigel Doggett, a saucy Brit, hopped aboard.
崭露头角的浪漫喜剧导演奈杰尔·多格特，一个俏皮的英国人，加入了进来。

343
00:18:30.549 --> 00:18:31.790
Nigel put me through
奈杰尔让我经历

344
00:18:31.790 --> 00:18:36.910
many rewrites, changed the story a bit, cast big movie stars that were nothing like me or Paige.
了多次重写，稍微改动了故事，还选了和我或佩吉一点也不像的大牌影星。

345
00:18:36.990 --> 00:18:38.741
Movie me was way more handsome.
电影里的我帅气得多。

346
00:18:38.741 --> 00:18:41.790
Movie Paige was not quite as pretty as the real thing,
电影里的佩吉没有真人那么漂亮，

347
00:18:41.790 --> 00:18:48.030
but the script got shot with its spirit mostly intact, rare, and became a bonafide hit, rarer.
但剧本拍出来了，其精神基本得以保留——这很罕见，并且成了名副其实的热门大片——这更罕见。

348
00:18:48.750 --> 00:18:53.550
I wasn't yet thirty, and suddenly I was an in-demand screenwriter who'd written a movie
我当时还不到三十岁，突然间我就成了一个炙手可热的编剧，写出了一部

349
00:18:53.550 --> 00:18:54.669
everybody had seen.
人人都看过的电影。

350
00:18:54.669 --> 00:18:58.910
It led to years of writing gigs, mostly rewriting other people's scripts
这带来了多年的编剧工作，主要都是重写别人的剧本

351
00:18:58.910 --> 00:19:02.919
or adapting a video game, comic book, or even a product into a movie.
或是把电子游戏、漫画书甚至产品改编成电影。

352
00:19:02.919 --> 00:19:04.430
No shame in it, that's the
这没什么好丢脸的，这就是

353
00:19:04.510 --> 00:19:07.053
bread and butter of a working screenwriter.
职业编剧的谋生饭碗。

354
00:19:07.053 --> 00:19:09.950
Good money, which it turned out we really needed.
报酬很丰厚，事实证明我们真的很需要钱。

355
00:19:11.150 --> 00:19:16.530
Because around the time Subway series was born, so was Emmet, and everything changed.
因为就在《地铁》系列诞生的时候，埃米特也出生了，一切都变了。

356
00:19:16.530 --> 00:19:16.910
Again,
再说一次，

357
00:19:17.630 --> 00:19:21.486
our baby boy had Down syndrome and would need open heart surgery.
我们的宝贝儿子患有唐氏综合征，需要进行开心手术。

358
00:19:21.486 --> 00:19:22.910
Over the next few years,
在接下来的几年里，

359
00:19:22.910 --> 00:19:26.331
life in Los Angeles became too hard, especially for Paige.
我们在洛杉矶的生活变得异常艰难，尤其是对佩姬而言。

360
00:19:26.331 --> 00:19:28.750
My job was to make stuff up for a living,
我的工作是靠凭空捏造谋生，

361
00:19:28.750 --> 00:19:30.677
her job was to keep our son alive.
她的工作则是维持我们儿子的生命。

362
00:19:30.677 --> 00:19:34.190
So we moved back East to be near family for the extra support.
所以我们搬回了东海岸，离家人近一点以获得更多的支持。

363
00:19:34.590 --> 00:19:38.590
But also because New York felt like home to Paige, in a way LA never would.
但也是因为对佩姬来说，纽约才真正有家的感觉，这是洛杉矶永远无法比拟的。

364
00:19:39.470 --> 00:19:42.670
She didn't want to have another baby in that city, and I understood that.
她不想在那座城市再生一个孩子，我理解这一点。

365
00:19:43.950 --> 00:19:46.377
But there was a cost to leaving Hollywood.
但离开好莱坞也是有代价的。

366
00:19:46.377 --> 00:19:48.110
My career steadily cooled off.
我的事业开始稳步走下坡路。

367
00:19:48.910 --> 00:19:51.936
I forestalled it for a while by flying back and forth a ton.
我通过频繁往返两地，勉强维持了一阵子。

368
00:19:51.936 --> 00:19:53.550
You should see my frequent flyer
你应该看看我的常客飞行

369
00:19:53.550 --> 00:19:53.969
miles.
里程。

370
00:19:53.969 --> 00:19:56.062
But out of sight, out of mind.
但眼不见，心不烦。

371
00:19:56.062 --> 00:19:59.550
I was three thousand miles away from every coffee,
我远在三千英里之外，错过了所有的咖啡局、

372
00:19:59.630 --> 00:20:02.307
breakfast, lunch, dinner, cocktails.
早餐、午餐、晚餐和鸡尾酒会。

373
00:20:02.307 --> 00:20:04.910
Jobs come from those, sad but true.
工作都是从这些场合来的，虽然残酷但却是事实。

374
00:20:05.870 --> 00:20:10.830
I still landed rewrite gigs on big studio movies, although I rarely received screen credit.
我仍然能接到大制片厂电影的剧本重写工作，尽管我很少能拿到署名权。

375
00:20:11.710 --> 00:20:16.910
Even with a sweeping page one rewrite, a writer usually gets cash but no credit.
哪怕进行了伤筋动骨的全面重写，编剧通常也只能拿到钱，却没有署名。

376
00:20:16.910 --> 00:20:20.597
You're a ghostwriter, paid to save the day then disappear.
你只是个代笔人，拿钱救场然后消失。

377
00:20:20.597 --> 00:20:21.550
I sold original
我也卖过原创的

378
00:20:21.550 --> 00:20:24.914
spec scripts too, some of which I loved, but none got made.
剧本，其中有些我很喜欢，但没有一部被拍出来。

379
00:20:24.914 --> 00:20:26.910
I was still known mostly for Subway
我依然主要因为《地铁》

380
00:20:26.910 --> 00:20:30.091
series, which ran on basic cable constantly.
系列而为人所知，这个剧集一直在基础有线电视频道上重播。

381
00:20:30.091 --> 00:20:31.030
Years passed.
几年过去了。

382
00:20:31.030 --> 00:20:32.910
The phone stopped ringing.
电话不再响了。

383
00:20:33.790 --> 00:20:36.686
And I slowly became what I'd always dreaded.
我慢慢变成了我一直恐惧的那种人。

384
00:20:36.686 --> 00:20:38.990
That guy who wrote that thing once.
那个曾经写过那个东西的家伙。

385
00:20:40.590 --> 00:20:43.084
Today's Subway series feels like an old movie.
如今的《地铁》系列感觉就像一部老电影。

386
00:20:43.084 --> 00:20:45.470
The characters talk on cell phones that look
角色们用的手机看起来

387
00:20:45.470 --> 00:20:48.612
gigantic, and they actually call each other.
巨大无比，而且他们居然还给彼此打电话。

388
00:20:48.612 --> 00:20:51.110
Who the fuck calls anybody anymore?
现在谁他妈还给别人打电话啊？

389
00:20:51.110 --> 00:20:52.110
The characters
剧中的角色

390
00:20:52.110 --> 00:20:52.942
don't text.
不发短信。

391
00:20:52.942 --> 00:20:56.195
They don't use social media or dating apps.
不用社交媒体，也不用约会软件。

392
00:20:56.195 --> 00:20:56.498
Why?
为什么？

393
00:20:56.498 --> 00:20:59.070
Because none of that shit existed.
因为那些破玩意儿当时根本不存在。

394
00:21:00.030 --> 00:21:01.394
Some of the dialogue is dated.
有些台词已经过时了。

395
00:21:01.394 --> 00:21:04.350
The characters tell jokes that aren't always politically correct.
角色们讲的笑话并不总是那么政治正确。

396
00:21:04.990 --> 00:21:09.550
The whole thing plays as a period piece now, a portrait of an era that has passed.
整部作品现在看起来就像是一部年代戏，一幅逝去时代的群像。

397
00:21:10.830 --> 00:21:12.023
I'm still proud of it.
我依然为此感到骄傲。

398
00:21:12.023 --> 00:21:15.710
It's not exactly what I wanted it to be, but it's cute and it works.
它虽然不完全是我想要的样子，但它很可爱，而且效果不错。

399
00:21:16.510 --> 00:21:18.127
Is it a bit cheesy?
它有点老套吗？

400
00:21:18.127 --> 00:21:18.467
Yes.
是的。

401
00:21:18.467 --> 00:21:20.510
But then again, so am I.
但话又说回来，我也是。

402
00:21:21.310 --> 00:21:23.924
My grand unified theory is this.
我的宏大统一理论是这样的。

403
00:21:23.924 --> 00:21:26.376
Deep down, people want cheesy.
内心里，人们都喜欢通俗狗血的东西。

404
00:21:26.376 --> 00:21:28.990
My father passed away very young
我父亲去世得很早

405
00:21:28.990 --> 00:21:32.033
while I was still in high school, and it made no sense.
当时我还在读高中，这完全说不通。

406
00:21:32.033 --> 00:21:34.190
I spent my teen years disappearing into
我的青少年时期都在

407
00:21:34.190 --> 00:21:38.348
movies, the kind that let you escape into something good.
电影中度过，那种能让你逃避到美好事物中的电影。

408
00:21:38.348 --> 00:21:39.952
I love a happy ending.
我喜欢大团圆结局。

409
00:21:39.952 --> 00:21:41.630
I'm not a fan of messy.
我不喜欢一团糟。

410
00:21:41.630 --> 00:21:43.472
I like it when things make sense.
我喜欢事情顺理成章。

411
00:21:43.472 --> 00:21:46.990
We all go to the movies to see a world where things make sense.
我们去看电影都是为了看到一个一切都顺理成章的世界。

412
00:21:47.870 --> 00:21:49.650
I write movies like that.
我写的就是那种电影。

413
00:21:49.650 --> 00:21:54.350
Some of Subway series memorable moments get memed online nowadays,
如今《地铁系列》的一些难忘时刻在网上成了表情包，

414
00:21:54.350 --> 00:21:59.630
especially the climactic line delivered by movie me to movie page right before their big kiss.
尤其是电影里的我和电影里的佩姬在他们热吻前夕说的那句高潮台词。

415
00:22:00.590 --> 00:22:03.926
You can't hit a home run if there's no home to run to.
如果没有本垒让你跑回去，你就无法打出本垒打。

416
00:22:03.926 --> 00:22:05.470
As with anything popular,
和任何热门事物一样，

417
00:22:05.470 --> 00:22:11.310
debates rage online about whether that line is a rom-com classic or absolute flaming shit.
网络上关于这句台词到底是浪漫喜剧经典还是彻头彻尾的烂泥，争论不休。

418
00:22:12.110 --> 00:22:14.323
But that's how I felt about Paige.
但这就是我对佩姬的感觉。

419
00:22:14.323 --> 00:22:15.950
Right away, she was home.
一见面，她就是我的归宿。

420
00:22:17.310 --> 00:22:21.950
Subway series became a part of our story, an iconic commemoration of our meat cute.
《地铁系列》成了我们故事的一部分，是我们浪漫相遇的标志性纪念。

421
00:22:22.590 --> 00:22:27.070
Whenever Paige and I hit a rough patch, I always had Subway series in my back pocket,
每当我和佩姬遇到感情危机，我总能把《地铁系列》当成杀手锏，

422
00:22:27.630 --> 00:22:33.550
my get out of jail free card, a love letter to my wife that somehow became a big hit movie.
我的免狱通行证，一封写给我妻子的情书，却不知怎么成了一部大卖的热门电影。

423
00:22:36.140 --> 00:22:39.633
Paige, I hate Subway series.
佩姬，我讨厌《地铁系列》。

424
00:22:39.633 --> 00:22:43.500
I've never told this to anyone.
我从来没对任何人说过这个。

425
00:22:44.140 --> 00:22:44.929
How could I?
怎么可能呢？

426
00:22:44.929 --> 00:22:50.060
What woman wouldn't want their partner to write a hit rom-com inspired by her?
哪个女人会不希望自己的伴侣为她写一部受她启发的热门浪漫喜剧呢？

427
00:22:50.860 --> 00:22:52.492
Look, I love Rob.
听着，我爱罗布。

428
00:22:52.492 --> 00:22:58.156
I love his enthusiasm, even when it flirts with cheesiness.
我喜欢他的热情，尽管有时显得有点老套和俗气。

429
00:22:58.156 --> 00:22:59.980
And I love the idea
我也喜欢

430
00:22:59.980 --> 00:23:05.260
of this movie being for me, which is why I've kept my mouth shut for two decades.
这部电影是为我而作的这种说法，这就是为什么我二十年来一直守口如瓶。

431
00:23:06.300 --> 00:23:09.645
But the truth is, I can barely sit through it.
但事实是，我几乎坐着看都看不下去。

432
00:23:09.645 --> 00:23:12.700
Rob likes to say it's a love letter to me,
罗布总爱说这是一封写给我的情书，

433
00:23:12.700 --> 00:23:13.480
but is it?
但真的是吗？

434
00:23:13.480 --> 00:23:19.100
If you look closely, Subway series is not really for or about me at all.
如果你仔细看，《地铁系列》其实根本不是为了我或关于我的。

435
00:23:20.540 --> 00:23:25.980
Sure, Rob and I are both big meds fans, and baseball is a part of how we met and fell in love.
当然，我和罗布都是大都会队的超级球迷，棒球也是我们相识相恋的一部分。

436
00:23:26.060 --> 00:23:30.380
For our first anniversary, he bought me a customized burn-shits-brewers jersey,
为了我们的第一个结婚纪念日，他给我买了一件定制的球衣，

437
00:23:30.380 --> 00:23:33.820
which is still, to this day, my single favorite possession.
直到今天，它仍然是我最珍视的私人物品。

438
00:23:34.380 --> 00:23:35.841
And we both hated the Yankees.
我们俩都讨厌扬基队。

439
00:23:35.841 --> 00:23:38.324
Metz fans are legally required to hate the Yankees.
大都会队球迷法律规定必须讨厌扬基队。

440
00:23:38.324 --> 00:23:38.860
Look it up,
不信去查查，

441
00:23:38.860 --> 00:23:40.938
it's in the Constitution.
宪法里写着呢。

442
00:23:40.938 --> 00:23:45.260
The Mets have won a grand total of two world series.
大都会队总共只赢过两次世界大赛。

443
00:23:46.220 --> 00:23:48.329
The Yankees have won 27.
扬基队赢了27次。

444
00:23:48.329 --> 00:23:50.086
We get to be better.
我们有资格表现得清高一点。

445
00:23:50.086 --> 00:23:51.580
It's all we have.
我们只有这个了。

446
00:23:52.460 --> 00:23:58.776
But in the movie, Rob made the me character a Yankees fan.
但在电影里，罗布却把以我为原型的角色设定成了扬基球迷。

447
00:23:58.776 --> 00:24:00.300
What the fuck?
搞什么鬼？

448
00:24:01.100 --> 00:24:03.477
To a Mets fan, that's sacrilege.
对大都会球迷来说，这简直是大逆不道。

449
00:24:03.477 --> 00:24:08.380
Plus, Rob never even showed me the script until after it was sold.
而且，罗布甚至直到电影卖出后才把剧本给我看。

450
00:24:08.380 --> 00:24:12.940
When I finally read it, I asked, wait, why am I a Yankees fan?
当我终于读完时，我问，等等，为什么我是扬基球迷？

451
00:24:13.660 --> 00:24:17.260
Oh, I needed to do that, he said, to add conflict.
“噢，我得这么写，”他说，“为了增加冲突。”

452
00:24:17.980 --> 00:24:19.646
Oh, well, you succeeded.
哦，好吧，你成功了。

453
00:24:19.646 --> 00:24:22.700
I found why couldn't you be the Yankees fan?
我发现，为什么你不能当洋基队的球迷？

454
00:24:23.420 --> 00:24:27.500
Of course, we both knew the real answer because Yankees fans are assholes.
当然，我们俩都知道真正的答案，因为洋基球迷都是混蛋。

455
00:24:27.500 --> 00:24:28.817
Sorry, but they are.
抱歉，但他们确实是。

456
00:24:28.817 --> 00:24:33.820
Yankees fans are the lazy, entitled nobility to Metz fans humble hardworking
洋基球迷是慵懒、享有特权的贵族，而大都会球迷则是谦逊、努力的

457
00:24:33.820 --> 00:24:34.514
peasantry.
底层百姓。

458
00:24:34.514 --> 00:24:36.805
The Yankees are Marie Antoinette.
洋基队是玛丽·安托瓦内特。

459
00:24:36.805 --> 00:24:38.540
The Mets are Joan of Arc.
大都会队是圣女贞德。

460
00:24:39.660 --> 00:24:42.123
I'll ask Nigel if we can switch it, Rob said.
罗布说：“我去问问奈杰尔能不能换一下。”

461
00:24:42.123 --> 00:24:42.780
No big deal.
没什么大不了的。

462
00:24:43.580 --> 00:24:45.070
But guess what happened?
但你猜怎么着？

463
00:24:45.070 --> 00:24:46.374
Have you guessed yet?
你猜到了吗？

464
00:24:46.374 --> 00:24:47.740
It never got switched.
它根本没换。

465
00:24:48.380 --> 00:24:51.900
He later said Nigel preferred the woman as a Yankees fan.
他后来又说，奈杰尔更倾向于让那个女人当洋基球迷。

466
00:24:52.540 --> 00:24:53.561
Why you ask?
你问为什么？

467
00:24:53.561 --> 00:24:55.858
I never got an explanation.
我从来没得到过解释。

468
00:24:55.858 --> 00:24:57.900
Because there isn't one.
因为根本就没有解释。

469
00:24:58.780 --> 00:25:03.980
The truth is, I'm not convinced Rob ever even pitched the idea of switching it.
事实是，我甚至不相信罗布真的提出过调换的建议。

470
00:25:05.340 --> 00:25:08.300
The most quoted line from Subway series is,
《地铁系列》里被引用最多的一句台词是：

471
00:25:09.260 --> 00:25:13.180
you can't hit a home run if there's no home to run to.
“如果没有可以跑回的本垒，你就无法击出本垒打。”

472
00:25:14.380 --> 00:25:22.380
So, in this formulation, the woman is home plate and her whole job is to just wait there
所以，在这种设定下，那个女人就是本垒板，她的全部工作就是等在那里

473
00:25:22.380 --> 00:25:25.107
for the man to complete his victory trot.
等男人完成他的胜利慢跑。

474
00:25:25.107 --> 00:25:27.900
Then step on her because that's not great.
然后踩在她头上，因为这可真不怎么样。

475
00:25:29.340 --> 00:25:35.900
When the movie came out, the tagline on the poster was, she was a catch, but there was a catch.
电影上映时，海报上的宣传语是：她是个好女孩，但有个小陷阱。

476
00:25:36.860 --> 00:25:40.417
Yeah, the catch is, she's a damn Yankees fan.
对啊，陷阱就是，她是个该死的洋基球迷。

477
00:25:40.417 --> 00:25:43.500
Also, the character is nothing like me.
而且，那个角色跟我一点也不像。

478
00:25:43.500 --> 00:25:45.153
She's much nicer than me.
她比我好得多。

479
00:25:45.153 --> 00:25:47.666
And I don't mean that as a compliment.
我这话可不是在夸奖。

480
00:25:47.666 --> 00:25:49.980
She feels less like an actual woman
比起一个真正的女人，她感觉更像

481
00:25:49.980 --> 00:25:52.536
and more like a woman written by a man.
是一个出自男人笔下的女性。

482
00:25:52.536 --> 00:25:54.044
I'll do respect to Rob.
出于对罗布的尊重。

483
00:25:54.044 --> 00:25:55.420
I know he meant well.
我知道他是好意。

484
00:25:56.300 --> 00:25:59.489
Everything she does is in reaction to the man.
她所做的一切都是对男人的反应。

485
00:25:59.489 --> 00:26:02.540
Being charmed, rolling her eyes, getting mad
被迷住、翻白眼、生气

486
00:26:02.620 --> 00:26:08.460
forgiving, all reactive based on a true love story, the poster claimed.
原谅，全都是基于一个真实爱情故事的被动反应，海报上这么声称。

487
00:26:09.020 --> 00:26:14.860
So then the whole world started asking me, how does it feel to see yourself on the big screen?
于是全世界开始问我：“在银幕上看到自己是什么感觉？”

488
00:26:15.500 --> 00:26:17.557
And I wanted to say, I have no idea.
我想说的是，我不知道。

489
00:26:17.557 --> 00:26:18.643
It's not really me.
那根本不是我。

490
00:26:18.643 --> 00:26:20.300
I don't even like that chick.
我甚至都不喜欢那个妞。

491
00:26:20.940 --> 00:26:22.101
But I couldn't.
但我不能这么说。

492
00:26:22.101 --> 00:26:25.817
By the time the movie came out, Emmett was born.
电影上映的时候，埃mett出生了。

493
00:26:25.817 --> 00:26:28.140
So simultaneously, Rob had two
所以与此同时，罗布有了两个

494
00:26:28.140 --> 00:26:28.646
babies.
宝贝。

495
00:26:28.646 --> 00:26:33.276
One, a sparkly hit rom-com that made him the toast of Hollywood.
一个是光芒四射、让他成为好莱坞宠儿的热门浪漫喜剧。

496
00:26:33.276 --> 00:26:34.940
The other a complicated
另一个是一个复杂

497
00:26:34.940 --> 00:26:37.041
mystery with an uncertain future.
且前途未卜的谜团。

498
00:26:37.041 --> 00:26:40.860
It should come as no surprise which one he leaned into more.
他更倾向于哪一个，这用脚趾头都能想出来。

499
00:26:41.660 --> 00:26:45.773
Rob Nigel and the cast traveled the globe to promote Subway series.
罗布、奈杰尔和演员们环游世界来宣传《地铁系列》。

500
00:26:45.773 --> 00:26:46.940
At every press junk
在每一场新闻发布会上

501
00:26:46.940 --> 00:26:49.354
at Rob said I was his inspiration.
罗布说我是他的灵感源泉。

502
00:26:49.354 --> 00:26:53.260
And the reporters and screening audiences would all go,
而记者和点映观众们都会发出这样的惊叹：

503
00:26:54.060 --> 00:26:58.118
oh, but I did not feel like an inspiration.
哦，但我一点也不觉得自己是什么灵感源泉。

504
00:26:58.118 --> 00:27:02.460
I was the new mother of a baby I did not fully
我是一个新手妈妈，面对着一个我并非完全

505
00:27:02.460 --> 00:27:03.331
understand.
了解的婴儿。

506
00:27:03.331 --> 00:27:06.734
Living in a city in which I felt all alone.
住在一个让我感到孤立无援的城市里。

507
00:27:06.734 --> 00:27:09.820
Watching my husband's fever for success
看着我丈夫对成功的狂热

508
00:27:09.820 --> 00:27:14.155
burn hotter than ever now that we had a special needs child.
烧得比以往任何时候都旺，尤其是在我们有了一个特殊需求儿童之后。

509
00:27:14.155 --> 00:27:16.540
Rob felt he had to become a super
罗布觉得他必须成为一个超级

510
00:27:16.540 --> 00:27:20.138
provider to support Emmett for his entire life.
顶梁柱，好支撑埃mett度过他的一生。

511
00:27:20.138 --> 00:27:23.660
The thing is Rob was a full-on workaholic even
问题是，罗布在埃mett出生之前

512
00:27:23.660 --> 00:27:25.493
before Emmett's birth.
就是一个十足的工作狂。

513
00:27:25.493 --> 00:27:31.660
But now our baby's needs gave him a free pass to not only continue working
但现在，我们宝宝的需求让他获得了一张免罪金牌，不仅可以继续

514
00:27:31.660 --> 00:27:37.018
non-stop, obsessively, but to do it all under this cloak of nobility.
没完没了、执着地工作，而且这一切都有了一层崇高的伪装。

515
00:27:37.018 --> 00:27:39.580
He was the hero, the breadwinner.
他是英雄，是顶梁柱。

516
00:27:40.540 --> 00:27:47.020
Rob loved Emmett, but he also wanted to be off having immersive adventures in imaginary lands,
罗布爱埃mett，但他也想抽身去虚拟世界里进行沉浸式的冒险，

517
00:27:47.020 --> 00:27:54.395
escaping the hard day-to-day realities I had to confront, mostly on my own.
逃避我不得不独自面对的残酷的日常生活现实。

518
00:27:54.395 --> 00:27:56.460
At such a lost lonely
在我这样一个迷茫孤独的

519
00:27:56.460 --> 00:28:04.380
moment for me watching Subway series, this shiny, happy rom-com populated by beautiful young actors
时刻，看着《地铁系列》，这部由年轻貌美的演员们主演、

520
00:28:04.380 --> 00:28:08.332
with fake accents, none of them were real New Yorkers.
带着假口音的光鲜亮丽的快乐浪漫喜剧，里面没有一个是真正的纽约客。

521
00:28:08.332 --> 00:28:11.260
Felt like seeing a mirage in the desert,
感觉就像在大沙漠里看到了海市蜃楼，

522
00:28:11.900 --> 00:28:18.095
a strange hallucination born of thirSt. None of it resembled my real life.
一种因极度渴望而诞生的奇异幻觉。这一切都不像我的真实生活。

523
00:28:18.095 --> 00:28:19.100
Not anymore.
再也不像了。

524
00:28:19.980 --> 00:28:25.001
It felt like the funhouse mirror version of a life I'd left behind.
感觉就像我抛弃的生活的哈哈镜版本。

525
00:28:25.001 --> 00:28:27.100
A fairy tale about two young
一个关于两个年轻人的童话故事

526
00:28:27.100 --> 00:28:33.148
people with no responsibilities, no crushing parental weight on their shoulders.
没有责任负担，肩膀上也没有沉重的父母压力。

527
00:28:33.148 --> 00:28:33.980
Nothing but
只有

528
00:28:33.980 --> 00:28:39.980
free time to obsess over baseball and drink beer and eat hot dogs and never gain weight.
痴迷棒球、喝啤酒、吃热狗且永不发胖的自由时间。

529
00:28:40.860 --> 00:28:44.639
This is why rom-coms are such an irresponsible genre.
这就是为什么浪漫喜剧如此不负责任。

530
00:28:44.639 --> 00:28:47.420
They make us chase illusions, then feel
它们让我们追逐幻想，一旦得到后

531
00:28:47.420 --> 00:28:49.510
bored and sad once we catch them.
又感到无聊和悲伤。

532
00:28:49.510 --> 00:28:50.460
God bless them.
上帝保佑它们。

533
00:28:50.460 --> 00:28:53.310
Rob is the gooey dreamer in our relationship.
罗布是我们关系中那个感性的梦想家。

534
00:28:53.310 --> 00:28:53.500
I'm
我是

535
00:28:53.500 --> 00:29:00.628
the pragmatiSt. The rom-com era of my life, if I'd ever had one, was over.
那个现实主义者。我生命中的浪漫喜剧时代，如果曾经有过的话，也已经结束了。

536
00:29:00.628 --> 00:29:00.917
No.
不。

537
00:29:00.917 --> 00:29:02.940
The movie I wanted to
我现在想看的

538
00:29:02.940 --> 00:29:06.621
see now was the one that happens after the credits.
电影是字幕滚动之后发生的故事。

539
00:29:06.621 --> 00:29:09.580
After the big proposal on the centerfield
在中外场

540
00:29:09.580 --> 00:29:16.060
jumbotron, after the charming, light-hearted characters get hit by a real-life wrecking ball,
大屏幕上的盛大求婚之后，在迷人轻松的角色们遭到现实生活重击之后，

541
00:29:16.060 --> 00:29:23.340
shattered, pulverized, forced to gather up the tiny pieces of their former selves and glue them
他们支离破碎、粉身碎骨，被迫收拾过去自己的微小碎片，把它们粘合

542
00:29:23.340 --> 00:29:28.940
into something new, letting go forever of the perfect lives they'd planned on having.
成崭新的事物，永远放弃他们原本计划拥有的完美生活。

543
00:29:29.900 --> 00:29:32.039
That's the movie I wanted to see.
那才是我真正想看的电影。

544
00:29:32.039 --> 00:29:35.020
But Hollywood doesn't make that kind of movie.
但好莱坞不拍这种电影。

545
00:29:36.220 --> 00:29:38.700
So, I wrote it as a book instead.
所以，我把它写成了一本书。

546
00:29:58.940 --> 00:30:08.860
You'll hear confidence, misplaced confidence, bold guesses, wrong answers,
你会听到自信、盲目的自信、大胆的猜测、错误的回答、

547
00:30:08.860 --> 00:30:13.420
quick laughs, and the slow realization that maybe this was a bad idea to say out loud.
爽朗的笑声，以及渐渐意识到大声说出这些可能不是个好主意的领悟。

548
00:30:13.420 --> 00:30:18.460
If you like smart comedy, sharp conversations, and trivia that exists purely to melt people's
如果你喜欢机智的喜剧、犀利的对话，以及纯粹为了把人们大脑

549
00:30:18.460 --> 00:30:21.897
brains, this is Funny You Ask with me, Ike Baronholz.
烧坏而存在的冷知识，欢迎收听由我艾克·巴伦霍尔茨主持的《你问得好》。

550
00:30:21.897 --> 00:30:24.620
Follow Funny You Ask with Ike Baronholz on
在各大平台关注由艾克·巴伦霍尔茨主持的《你问得好》

551
00:30:24.620 --> 00:30:27.900
Spotify, Apple Podcast, or wherever you listen to podcasts.
在 Spotify、Apple Podcast 或你收听播客的任何地方收听。

552
00:30:29.420 --> 00:30:31.660
Every now and then, someone would die.
时不时地，就会有人死去。

553
00:30:32.540 --> 00:30:34.962
Risk, true stories, no holding back.
《Risk》，真实的故事，毫无保留。

554
00:30:34.962 --> 00:30:38.460
It comes up behind me, and whispers hard in the air.
它从我身后逼近，在空气中狠狠地低语。

555
00:30:39.260 --> 00:30:41.319
Nobody knows where you are.
没人知道你在哪。

556
00:30:41.319 --> 00:30:45.970
Some of these stories will make you laugh till you're crying.
其中一些故事会让你笑得眼泪直流。

557
00:30:45.970 --> 00:30:49.419
And then I realize I'm unconscious.
然后我意识到我失去了知觉。

558
00:30:49.419 --> 00:30:52.770
Some will stick with you for life.
有些故事会让你铭记一生。

559
00:30:52.770 --> 00:30:55.159
They catch my mom poisoning my sister.
他们拍下了我妈妈毒害我姐姐的画面。

560
00:30:55.159 --> 00:30:58.050
My brother, he died at the hands of my mother.
我哥哥，他死在我母亲的手里。

561
00:30:58.050 --> 00:31:00.686
I said, Mike, don't make me shoot your place.
我说，迈克，别逼我朝你的地方开枪。

562
00:31:00.686 --> 00:31:01.330
But he did.
但他还是这么做了。

563
00:31:01.330 --> 00:31:05.730
People say risk makes them feel less alone and more alive.
人们说《Risk》让他们感到不再孤单，而且更有活力的。

564
00:31:05.730 --> 00:31:09.410
During my time in prison, I discovered a podcast called Risk.
在我服刑期间，我发现了一个叫《Risk》的播客。

565
00:31:10.130 --> 00:31:13.490
That podcast saved my life so many times.
那个播客无数次拯救了我的生命。

566
00:31:14.210 --> 00:31:18.930
Live your authentic truth, because you never know who may come to your funeral and turn that
活出你真实的自我，因为你永远不知道谁会来到你的葬礼上，然后把那个

567
00:31:19.010 --> 00:31:19.759
bitch out.
贱人赶出去。

568
00:31:19.759 --> 00:31:26.050
Risk, the show where people tell true stories they never thought they dare to share,
《Risk》，一个让人们讲述他们从未想过敢于分享的真实故事的节目，

569
00:31:26.050 --> 00:31:29.375
wherever you get your podcasts.
在你获取播客的地方都可以收听。

570
00:31:29.375 --> 00:31:33.128
Darcy, I'm barely in my mom's book.
达西，我几乎没怎么出现在我妈写的书里。

571
00:31:33.128 --> 00:31:36.560
That should tell you everything.
光是这一点就说明了一切。

572
00:31:37.680 --> 00:31:41.443
I hate to be the cliche sibling of a child with a disability.
我讨厌成为那种家里有残疾儿童的落入俗套的手足。

573
00:31:41.443 --> 00:31:42.800
But when you're a kid,
但当你还是个孩子的时候，

574
00:31:42.800 --> 00:31:47.920
and you get less attention than your brother, and you don't fully understand why, it's hard.
当你得到的关注比你哥哥少，而且你又不是完全理解原因时，这很难受。

575
00:31:48.720 --> 00:31:51.520
It took me a while to realize Emmett was different.
我花了好一阵子才意识到埃mett 与众不同。

576
00:31:52.320 --> 00:31:55.760
Around age six, I noticed that kids sometimes made fun of him.
大约六岁的时候，我注意到孩子们有时会取笑他。

577
00:31:56.640 --> 00:31:59.108
Then I heard this weird word I didn't know.
然后我听到这个我不认识的奇怪词汇。

578
00:31:59.108 --> 00:32:01.920
Before that word, my brother was just my brother.
在这个词出现之前，我哥哥就只是我哥哥。

579
00:32:02.720 --> 00:32:03.298
Funny?
有趣？

580
00:32:03.298 --> 00:32:03.876
Silly?
滑稽？

581
00:32:03.876 --> 00:32:06.283
A bit eccentric, I guess?
我猜有点古怪？

582
00:32:06.283 --> 00:32:07.920
But normal to me.
但在我看来很正常。

583
00:32:08.800 --> 00:32:12.720
If he was being annoying, I'd say, stop Emmett, you're being annoying.
如果他很烦人，我会说，别这样，埃mett，你很烦人。

584
00:32:13.680 --> 00:32:15.952
But mostly I just looked up to him.
但大多数时候我只是崇拜他。

585
00:32:15.952 --> 00:32:17.120
He was my big bro.
他是我的好大哥。

586
00:32:18.400 --> 00:32:22.413
Then, one day on the school bus, he was a retard.
然后，有一天在校车上，他成了个智障。

587
00:32:22.413 --> 00:32:25.280
I asked my parents what that meant.
我问我父母那是什么意思。

588
00:32:26.080 --> 00:32:28.080
I'll never forget the looks on their faces.
我永远不会忘记他们脸上的表情。

589
00:32:29.040 --> 00:32:31.680
Got it, but also like they'd been waiting for it.
明白了，但也像是他们一直在等这一刻。

590
00:32:32.720 --> 00:32:36.880
They said sometimes bullies used that word to be mean to people like my brother.
他们说有时霸凌者会用这个词来对付像我哥哥这样的人。

591
00:32:37.920 --> 00:32:40.551
Why would anyone want to be mean to Emmett?
为什么会有人想对埃mett刻薄？

592
00:32:40.551 --> 00:32:41.040
I asked.
我问。

593
00:32:42.000 --> 00:32:44.400
Because he's different, they said.
因为他与众不同，他们说。

594
00:32:45.360 --> 00:32:50.000
And that's when my parents gave me an in-depth description of what it really means to have Down
也就是在那时，我父母向我详细描述了患有唐氏

595
00:32:50.000 --> 00:32:56.560
Syndrome, the medical issues, the learning challenges, the social differences, and how it can make you
综合征的真正含义、医疗问题、学习挑战、社交差异，以及它如何使你

596
00:32:56.560 --> 00:32:58.511
a target for bullies.
成为霸凌的目标。

597
00:32:58.511 --> 00:32:59.997
I was so pissed.
我气坏了。

598
00:32:59.997 --> 00:33:01.670
Emmett was unique.
埃mett 是独一无二的。

599
00:33:01.670 --> 00:33:03.528
He was his own song.
他有着自己独特的旋律。

600
00:33:03.528 --> 00:33:05.200
If people couldn't
如果人们不能

601
00:33:05.280 --> 00:33:07.221
hear it, then fuck them.
听好了，然后去他妈的。

602
00:33:07.221 --> 00:33:11.588
On that day, I anointed myself my brother's protector.
在那一天，我自封为我哥哥的守护者。

603
00:33:11.588 --> 00:33:12.720
Emmett's kid's
埃mett的孩子...

604
00:33:12.720 --> 00:33:17.360
sister, half a decade younger, this little pipsqueak full of piss and vinegar,
妹妹，小他半个世纪，这个乳臭未干、气势汹汹的小不点，

605
00:33:17.840 --> 00:33:20.278
fending off the bullies, making a scene.
击退恶霸，大闹一场。

606
00:33:20.278 --> 00:33:21.680
The louder, the better.
声音越大越好。

607
00:33:22.640 --> 00:33:28.320
Once, when I was eight, I stood up on my seat and announced to the entire bus that Devano shields,
有一次，在我八岁的时候，我站在座位上，向整辆公车宣布德瓦诺·谢尔兹，

608
00:33:28.880 --> 00:33:32.004
who'd been blowing spitballs at a fast asleep Emmett.
他一直在向熟睡的埃梅特吐纸团。

609
00:33:32.004 --> 00:33:34.480
He always falls asleep in moving vehicles,
他坐移动的交通工具总是睡着，

610
00:33:34.480 --> 00:33:38.480
still does, was a fascist motherfucker who must be stopped.
现在还是这样，是个必须被阻止的法西斯他妈的混蛋。

611
00:33:39.840 --> 00:33:44.191
My parents beamed with pride as I regaled them with my tail of bravery.
当我向他们讲述我的英勇事迹时，我的父母骄傲得合不拢嘴。

612
00:33:44.191 --> 00:33:45.600
They quote that line to
他们直到今天

613
00:33:45.600 --> 00:33:50.560
this day to describe all kinds of people, from drivers who don't use their turn signal to
还引用那句话来形容各种各样的人，从不打转向灯的司机到

614
00:33:50.560 --> 00:33:51.575
certain U.S.
某些美国

615
00:33:51.575 --> 00:33:52.505
presidents.
总统。

616
00:33:52.505 --> 00:33:55.464
Hell yeah, they said, hi-fiving me.
太对了，他们说，与我击掌。

617
00:33:55.464 --> 00:33:58.000
I can still remember my mother
我至今仍能记住母亲

618
00:33:58.000 --> 00:34:04.160
squeezing me tight, whispering in my ear, Darcy, we are so lucky you're our daughter,
紧紧抱住我，在我耳边呢喃，达西，我们有你这样的女儿真是太幸运了，

619
00:34:04.160 --> 00:34:07.158
and Emmett is so lucky you're his sister.
埃梅特有你这样的妹妹真是太幸运了。

620
00:34:07.158 --> 00:34:09.351
I finally had their attention.
我终于引起了他们的注意。

621
00:34:09.351 --> 00:34:10.960
I finally had a thing.
我终于有了一样属于我的东西。

622
00:34:11.840 --> 00:34:17.871
Now, instead of living in Emmett's shadow, I was Emmett's bodyguard.
现在，我不再活在埃梅特的阴影下，而是成了埃梅特的保镖。

623
00:34:17.871 --> 00:34:20.000
Basking in my own glory,
沐浴在自己的光芒中，

624
00:34:20.000 --> 00:34:23.760
I climbed up to my favorite spot, the treehouse in our backyard.
我爬上了我最喜欢的地方，后院的树屋。

625
00:34:25.120 --> 00:34:27.404
It was Emmett's favorite spot, too.
这也是埃梅特最喜欢的地方。

626
00:34:27.404 --> 00:34:30.080
Whenever we played hide-and-seek as kids,
小时候我们玩捉迷藏时，

627
00:34:30.080 --> 00:34:32.880
he would announce, I'm going to hide in the treehouse,
他总是宣布，我要藏在树屋里，

628
00:34:33.520 --> 00:34:37.680
which kind of defeated the whole hide-and-seek concept but was also a classic Emmett.
这有点违背了捉迷藏的初衷，但也很有埃梅特的风格。

629
00:34:38.480 --> 00:34:41.227
He simply couldn't lie, still can't.
他就是不会撒谎，现在也不会。

630
00:34:41.227 --> 00:34:43.440
Emmett climbed up to find me.
埃梅特爬上来找我。

631
00:34:44.400 --> 00:34:46.678
Hey, I said, still a glow.
嘿，我说，余热未消。

632
00:34:46.678 --> 00:34:49.920
Hey, he said, climbing in next to me.
嘿，他说着，钻进我旁边。

633
00:34:50.880 --> 00:34:55.357
Listen, Darcy, don't do that anymore, okay?
听着，达西，以后别那样了，好吗？

634
00:34:55.357 --> 00:34:56.814
